==== script 169
// Corrected at release 0.8.0 (step 20i). A truncated distribution took the probability between
// a bound and a point as the difference of two lower tails. Close to a bound the two are nearly
// equal and the difference kept few digits: for a normal truncated to [0, 2], `cdf(1.196288e-12)`
// gave 1.00007e-12 where 1e-12 is right, and `quantile(1e-12)` was wrong by as much. An interval
// far in the upper tail, where both lower tails print as 1, was refused as holding no
// probability. The probability is now taken on the side where the tails are small, and from the
// integral of the density where they are still nearly equal. No rows.
let d = dist.truncated(dist.normal(), lower: 0, upper: 2)
d.cdf(1.196288e-12)
d.quantile(1e-12)
d.cdf(2 - 1e-9, upper: true)
dist.truncated(dist.normal(), lower: 9).quantile(0.5)
dist.truncated(dist.exponential(rate: 2), lower: 30, upper: 31).cdf(30.5)
dist.truncated(dist.poisson(lambda: 3), lower: 40).pdf(40)
==== script 170
// Corrected at release 0.8.0 (step 20n). A count distribution truncated to an interval whose
// lower bound stood a hair above a whole number, as `lower: 1e-10, upper: 0.5`, ended in an
// internal error at every quantile and every draw. The probability of the interval counted 0,
// since a count distribution reads 1e-10 as 0, while the quantile searched from the next whole
// number up, 1, to the last, 0. The nightly fuzz target found it. A bound within 1e-7 of a
// whole number now counts as that number in every function of the truncation, and a quantile
// is always a whole number that the interval keeps. No rows.
let d = dist.truncated(dist.poisson(lambda: 3), lower: 1e-10, upper: 0.5)
d.quantile(0.5)
d.quantile(0)
d.quantile(1)
d.pdf(0)
d.draw(3, seed: 1)
dist.truncated(dist.poisson(lambda: 7.1e-304), lower: 4e-323, upper: 2.1e-314).draw(2, seed: 1)
let b = dist.truncated(dist.binomial(n: 10, p: 0.5), lower: 2.5, upper: 20.5)
b.quantile(0)
b.quantile(1)
b.pdf(3)
